> > > > > Intuitively this makes sense.  Instead of just comparing the
> > > > > center of
> > > > > predicted values to the observed values, we can compare at the
> > > > > entire
> > > > > predictive distribution to the observed value.
> > > > >
> > > > > However, I'm a bit stuck on  how to actually calculate this in
> > > > > R.
> > > > >
> > > > > I can't figure out how to calculate the first term of the left
> > > > > hand
> > > > > side
> > > > > Screen Shot 2022-09-14 at 12.08.52.png
> > > > >
> > > > > The dnorm() command in R requires both a mean and standard
> > > > > deviation.
> > > > > INLA supplies 48 points from the marginal fitted
> > > > > distribution.
> > > > > How do
> > > > > I calculate the above values with those points?
> > > > >
> > > > > Alternately:  Perhaps I'm going about this the "long way",
> > > > > when
> > > > > there
