Related to this question, if I’ve passed a function through inla.posterior.sample.eval to get predicted responses for say y1-y0, where y1 is with exposure to variable X, and y0 is without exposure to variable X (many thanks again to H and team for help with that formula), and let’s also say I’m interested only in a subset of those responses for certain space-time units, can I assume the row numbers of the responses match the row numbers of my dataset going into the model?  I get the correct total number of observations but wasn’t certain about their ordering.

Thank you!
Katie

> On Sep 21, 2022, at 6:02 PM, Finn Lindgren <finn.l...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> ﻿Precisely. Like for a y~Poisson(lambda) model, samples from inla.posterior.sample gives you the information about the posterior distribution of lambda. But if you need a quantity that relates y1~Poisson(lambda1) to y2~Poisson(lambda2) (with y1 and y2 conditionally independent given the lambda values, you may need to sample y1 and y2 as well. E.g. if you’re interested in the posterior distribution of (y1-y2)^2.

> And also if you need posterior probabilities for y1; P(y1=k|data)=E(P(y1=k|lambda1)), where the expectation is over lambda1 ~ posterior distribution for lambda1.
>
> Finn
>
> > On 21 Sep 2022, at 22:52, JJ Hubbard <jordanj...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > ﻿Thank you Finn,
> >
> > Could you please explain what a response level sample means? Does that refer to a final prediction of the response variable?
> >
> > On Wednesday, September 21, 2022 at 2:48:21 PM UTC-7 finn.l...@gmail.com wrote:
> > > Inla.posterior.sample sample from the joint posterior distribution, and rmarginal only from the marginals. Anything that relies on the joint properties (such as relative orderings) would need to use inla.posterior.sample.
> > > Also not that boy sampling methods only sample the model parameters and predictor values; if you need response level samples you have to add that as an additional step yourself.
> > >
> > > Finn
> > >
> > > > On 21 Sep 2022, at 21:40, JJ Hubbard <jordanj...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > >
> > > > ﻿Hello,
> > > >
> > > > I have fit a model to a data where observations are grouped together. I would like to sample the marginal distributions and rank the observations in each group, repeat this process many times, and use the frequency of a given observation being in rank r as predicted probabilities for that rank.
> > > >
> > > > I have been doing this by calling inla.rmarginal(n, model$marginals.fitted.values[[i] for all i in my dataframe. Is this the best way to do so?
> > > >
> > > > How does inla.posterior.sample differ?
> > > >
> > > > Thanks,
> > > >
> > > > JJ
